ike mccarthy and  you know what  it it was he did and that's how he  handled it and instead of having a a  man-to-man conversation with the head  coach and resolving their issues and  moving forward and realizing that both  of their legacies are tied to working  together and winning games and pursuing  championships that they're either in it  together or they're all going to fail he  opted not to solve the problem and there  are plenty of people responsible in  green bay all the way up to mark murphy  for letting it fester as long as it did  but think of how many championships this  organization would have  if they had everyone on the same page  working in the right direction to be the  best team they could be and the one  overarching takeaway from all of this is  there was so much dysfunction in green  bay over most of the last decade they  just couldn't they couldn't even get  back to the super bowl and they're lucky  they made it at the playoffs eight  straight years  you know there's one thing that that  bothers me when we talk about  think of how many super bowls they would  have won and things like that  the thing that bothers me about that is  that for the most part  if the packers had gotten to multiple  super bowls i mean if they had if they  had gotten to a few of them  more than likely they would have had to  beat new england to win the super bowl  there is this assumption  that aaron rodgers pick a number out of  the sky should have won four super bowls  by now or three or some number more than  one  and and i get it i  i get it i don't want to sound like joe  biden here i get it i get it but i get  what you're saying  the problem with that is that there are  31 other teams the packers don't exist  in a vacuum it's easy to say oh my god  they should have won four super bowls  but but let's be realistic about that  you've got to beat the best coach of  this era and the best quarterback of  this era and maybe of all time  what is that easy  and and so that's one of the things that  sort of gets my goat about all these  discussions but you know right now  in the aftermath of this the packers  have to take a very hard look at where  they are as an organization and aaron  rodgers has to take a hard look at him  if he doesn't do it you know he's  missing the boat on this in my opinion  yeah and peter look i i get what you're  saying that there's no guarantee you're  going to win the super bowl if you get  there but 2011 they were 15-1 the table  was set for home field advantage at  lambeau field and they got thumped by  the giants who beat the patriots that  year in the super bowl and in 2014  they're in seattle in command of the  game and they can't string together the  situational football necessary to win  the game starting with was it morgan  burnett who who inexplicably took a knee  after intercepting a pass when he could  have scored another touchdown and made  the margin even bigger than it was so  it's a little thing  i'm a firm believer that  football is so inherently complex when  it comes down to 11 guys trying to do  what they're supposed to do with 11  other guys doing the exact opposite  you've got to be buttoned up at every  level and you've got to have everybody  clicking and working together so in  those moments where games are won or  lost the thing that's going to happen  that's going to cause you to win is more  likely to occur and contrast that with  the new england patriots even though  they have issues from time to time  everybody does their job everybody does  what they're supposed to do and they  consistently get the most out of the  talent they have and even if it's one  more super bowl two more three more  whatever  there is a clear sense that this packers  team did not get everything it could  have gotten out of the presence of one  of the great quarterbacks of all time  whether that's mike mccarthy's blame ted  thompson's blame aaron rodgers blame  mark murphy's blame or a little bit on  all 